<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Campaign</p><h1 class="enc-title">Guadalcanal campaign</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1942">1942</a>–<a href="/years/1943">1943</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Guadalcanal · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Guadalcanal campaign was a prolonged Allied offensive in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon-islands">Solomon Islands</a> from 1942 to 1943 during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>. Fought on land, at sea and in the air, it ended with Japanese withdrawal from the island. For the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empire-of-japan">Empire of Japan</a> it was a costly defeat that marked the shift of the Pacific war towards the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/guadalcanal-campaign.jpg" alt="Guadalcanal campaign" width="320" height="231" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Marines_rest_in_the_field_on_Guadalcanal.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After the attack on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attack-on-pearl-harbor">Pearl Harbor</a> on 7 December 1941, Japanese forces captured the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malaya">Malaya</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-indies">Dutch East Indies</a>, Wake Island, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilbert-islands">Gilbert Islands</a>, New Britain and Guam. In summer 1942 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> decided to mount offensives in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-guinea">New Guinea</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon-islands">Solomon Islands</a>, to defend the sea lines to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> and eventually to attack the major Japanese base at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rabaul">Rabaul</a>. The campaign followed the Allied defensive successes at the Battle of the Coral Sea and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-midway">Battle of Midway</a> in May and June 1942.</p><p>Allied forces were under Robert L. Ghormley, reporting to Chester W. Nimitz, and Major General Alexander Vandegrift's 1st Marine Division moved from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a>. The Japanese defence rested on the Combined Fleet under Isoroku Yamamoto and the Seventeenth Army under Harukichi Hyakutake.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>On 7 August 1942 mainly U.S. Marines landed on Guadalcanal, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tulagi">Tulagi</a> and Florida Island, taking the Japanese defenders by surprise. Within days a Japanese force of seven cruisers and one destroyer under Vice Admiral Gunichi Mikawa defeated Allied screening ships at the Battle of Savo Island. The Marines formed a perimeter around Lunga Point and finished the airfield, named Henderson Field on 12 August. Japanese attempts to retake it came in mid-September and in late October.</p><p>The Japanese brought troops by fast destroyers at night, the runs the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> called the Tokyo Express, while American aircraft at Henderson Field controlled the daytime. Naval battles followed, including the Eastern Solomons, Cape Esperance and the Santa Cruz Islands, and the Allies won the Naval Battle of Guadalcanal in mid-November. Japanese troops were short of supplies by late November. On 12 December the Japanese Navy proposed abandoning Guadalcanal, and imperial headquarters agreed on 26 December. In January 1943 the U.S. XIV Corps under Alexander Patch took over operations, and the last Japanese forces were evacuated by 9 February 1943.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Guadalcanal was the first major Allied land offensive against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. With the battles at Milne Bay and Buna–Gona on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-guinea">New Guinea</a>, it marked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a>' change from defence to offence and passed the strategic initiative to them. Failure to win air superiority forced Japan to rely on barges, destroyers and submarines for reinforcement, and the campaign strained the resources of both sides.</p><p>Afterwards Guadalcanal and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tulagi">Tulagi</a> became major Allied bases, with extra fighter and bomber airfields and large port facilities for the advance up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomons</a>. The campaign drew many war correspondents, among them Richard Tregaskis, whose Guadalcanal Diary was a bestseller in 1943.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Guadalcanal campaign” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Battle of Midway in June 1942, which cost Japan four fleet carriers, and the <b>Guadalcanal campaign</b> of August 1942 to February 1943 surrendered the strategic initiative to the United States for good.</blockquote><a href="/empire/empire-of-japan">Empire of Japan · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Japan invaded in 1942, and the <b>Guadalcanal campaign</b> of 1942 and 1943 turned the war in the Pacific.</blockquote><a href="/state/british-solomon-islands-protectorate">British Solomon Islands Protectorate</a></li></ul></section>
